INT. ALPINE HUT - MORNING

A one-room stone refuge at the edge of a glacier. Pine rafters, a cast-iron stove, and four bunks in a row. Frost on the inside of the window. Sunlight, sudden and blazing, pours through it onto a table laid with oatmeal and tin mugs.

Four climbers have just woken. VASILIKI KOUROS, 30s, wiry and sun-darkened, stirs the pot with a wooden spoon. TIBOR HALÁSZ, 50s, gray-bearded, gaunt, laces his boots with slow concentration. The youngest, JACARANDA FENTON-MBEKI, 19, a rope bag over her shoulder, stands at the window, grinning at the sky. AUGUSTINE "GUS" CALDER, 40s, their guide, sits on a bunk, back to the wall, oddly still.

The mountain outside is flawless. Not a cloud. Every ridge cut sharp as glass against the blue.

JACARANDA
(whispering with delight)
Look at it.

VASILIKI
I'm looking.

They eat. Spoons clink. The stove ticks. For a long minute it is perfect: the quiet pleasure of being alive. Only Gus does not eat. He is watching the window with the expression of a man reading a letter he does not want to finish.

TIBOR
Gus. Eat.

GUS
In a minute.

He rises, crosses to the window, and presses his palm to the glass. Far away, on the western horizon, a faint white line has drawn itself across the blue, thin as a hair, and it is growing.
